the US dollar dominates global trade in  2019 some 88% of international  transactions involved US dollars no  other currencies came close that gives  the u.s. extraordinary power over nearly  anyone who imports or exports anything  anywhere commodities like oil gold and  coffee are all priced in dollars  regardless of where they come from but  this cloud has long frustrated America's  rivals making them vulnerable to US  trade sanctions here's how the global  economy runs on the US dollar and why  some countries are developing  alternative systems that could chip away  at the dollars dominance the dollar came  to dominate trade after World War two  the US was the biggest economy in the  world other countries were trying to  rebuild and the dollar it was stable and  plentiful in 1944 a conference of 44  nations agreed to peg their currencies  to the US dollar while the dollar itself  was pegged to gold as global trade grew  so did the use of the dollar to conduct  the world's business even after the  United States abandoned the gold  standard in 1971 the dollar remained the  world's currency of choice the world has  gotten used to doing business in dollars  because it's just easier than doing  business in any other currency the  dollar is incredibly liquid meaning it's  easy to buy and sell things all over the  world the US banking system is very  efficient and those things combine to  make it cheaper for businesses to buy  and sell in dollars here's how a typical  international transaction works in u.s.  dollars a Canadian lumber company sells  boards to a French home builder the  buyers bank in France and the sellers  bank in Canada settled a payment in  dollars via correspondent banks in the  United States these correspondent banks  have accounts at the United States  Federal Reserve the money is transferred  seamlessly between these banks Fed  accounts because their status as  correspondent banks means they are seen  as safe counterparties the United States  says the use of these correspondent  banks means every transaction  technically touches US soil giving it  legal jurisdiction and compelling  foreign countries to abide by its laws  on money laundering  in corruption after 9/11 the u.s. used  the power of the dollar to advance its  foreign policy goals the idea was to cut  out the sources of funding for terror  organizations the u.s. used its control  of the dollar to increase surveillance  of global money flows and curb financing  toward bad actors it did this by  imposing sanctions on its rivals under  the system if a business or country  tries to trade with a sanctioned entity  in dollars the US has the power to cut  off its access to US currency but other  countries have been building workarounds  some EU countries oppose the US  sanctions against Iran  these sanctions put in place after the  u.s. withdrew from the Iranian nuclear  deal in 2018 included banning dollar  transactions with Iranian banks as a  result the EU is developing a euro back  system that is designed to facilitate  trade between Europe and Iran without  sending money across borders the EU  countries still in the Iran deal are  trying to figure out a way to work  around the u.s. system so they can trade  with Iran now that bothers the US which  is trying to exert maximum pressure on  the Iranian economy India already has an  alternative payment system in place that  it's using to do business with Iran  according to a Wall Street Journal  investigation it's been used to  facilitate transactions with sanctioned  Iranian entities but the United States  is still exerting its influence in the  Middle East through the power of the US  dollar the dollar still dominates global  trade you can see that in the standoff  between the u.s. and Iraq Iraq says they  want to throw us troops out of the  country the u.s. said we'll cut you off  from your dollars in the US system but  in the future it's not clear how strong  that threat will remain as other  countries chip away at the power of the  dollar if the power of the dollar falls  it could hurt the United States his  ability to control the global trading  system  you  [Music]
 

